e to the curve $y=\sqrt{x^{3}}-1$ at the point $(4,7)$ intersects the $x$-axis.

The intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values."] ["2ed6c176-c8ce-5e49-a7ef-0346fa82cc02" :question/worked-solution "Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=x^{\frac{3}{2}} - 1, \\f'(x)&=\frac{3 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}{2}=\frac{3 \sqrt{x}}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(4)&=\frac{3 \sqrt{(4)}}{2}=3, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(3)}=- \frac{1}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(4,7)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(7)&=- \frac{1}{3}(x-(4)), \\y&=- \frac{x}{3} + \frac{25}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=- \frac{1}{3}x+\left(\frac{25}{3}\right), \\x&=25.
